w much of a excellent screen you do get. \n Speaker 2:You know. \n Speaker 2:Yeah. \n Speaker 1:You'd\u2014 you'd have to sort of keep it down to a black and white L.C.D. menu or something. \n Speaker 0:Yeah. \n Speaker 0:Is it possible that\u2014 that for the older generation you could have like an extra button that you press for larger print, like you do in larger print books? \n Speaker 2:Did it say that? \n Speaker 0:Oh, I\u2014 it's not that far to the back, actually. \n Speaker 2:Teletext. \n Speaker 0:Just maybe less on the screen, but as long as they can read it, that's the main thing. \n Speaker 2:Yeah.Or what about kind of a dual function in that you've got the basic buttons just for your play, volume, programme, things, and also\u2014 and then a menu to go into with obvious pictures, obvious symbols, and that's where you control recording and things like that. \n Speaker 1:Mm-hmm. \n Speaker 0:Yes. \n Speaker 1:Mm-hmm. \n Speaker 0:The other thing is just ta\u2014 tackling into mobile phone design features again.You can have a flip top remote control, so that when you flip over the top, your screen is\u2014 you can have a bigger screen in the\u2014 when you flip over. \n Speaker 1:Yeah. \n Speaker 2:I think that's a cost thing. \n Speaker 0:Yeah. \n Speaker 2:I don't\u2014 we don't know how much we're gonna know about\u2014 \n Speaker 1:Yeah. \n Speaker 0:You don't know. \n Speaker 1:And like say, lot of little spaces, we\u2014 we sort of bu\u2014 bulk it, make it small. \n Speaker 2:Mm-hmm. \n Speaker 0:Yes, mm-hmm. \n Speaker 2:Yeah. \n Speaker 1:But it might have its cost implications. \n Speaker 0:And there's an\u2014",
